---
title: Cowboy Bebop
subject: Shinichirō Watanabe
genre: Anime
rating: 5
date: 2026-08-20
excerpt: >-
  Jazz-scored fatalism dressed up as a bounty-hunting procedural. Every episode
  ends like a held chord.
---

Twenty-six episodes, and almost none of them are about the bounty. The job is a pretext — a way to put four people who cannot stand still into a room together and see what they avoid talking about. Watanabe understood that an episodic structure is not a limitation but a rhythm section: same tempo, different solo each week.

Yoko Kanno's score does an unfair amount of the work, and I mean that as praise. The music is not underneath the show, it is the show's nervous system. A hard-bop horn line tells you a scene is a joke; a solo piano tells you someone is about to be honest; the church organ in the final stretch tells you the joke is over.

> Whatever happens, happens.

What ages best is the restraint. Spike's past leaks out in fragments across a full season, never explained in a monologue, never flashbacked into tidiness. Faye's amnesia resolves into something sadder than mystery. Jet gets one bonsai tree and one ex-girlfriend and somehow becomes the most legible adult in the cast. The show trusts an audience to assemble people out of gestures.

The last two episodes stop pretending this was ever a comedy. They are not cathartic and are not meant to be — the whole series has been about people postponing the moment their history catches up, and it always does. See you, space cowboy: still the only sign-off in television that earns its own melancholy.
